Titre$ X E R O C O M U S  ss. str.   -   M GELARDI - 2011
Com$ Bol. AMER 84 (XXVIII):34 - Adapté par Paul Bertéa 04-09-2011

1,0 $ Both pileus and stipe yellowish-red to reddish-brown, stipe deeply rooting, basal mycelium whitish to pale yellow, apparently growing only under Populus spp $ X. silwoodensis
1,2 $ Different colours and/or different host trees

2,3 $ Context white to whitish and unchangeable or very slightly flushing pale rose or vinaceous on cutting, stipe enlarging downwards at maturity, basal mycelium vivid yellow, mainly associated with conifers but also broadleaved trees and arctic/alpine shrubs in temperate (sub)montane woodlands
2,4 $ Context yellowish to bright yellow or golden yellow, associated with deciduous trees in lowland or mediterranean environment

3,0 $ Pileus bright apple green or grass green, context streaked by silver veins $ X. ferrugineus f. citrinovirens
3,0 $ Pileus olive-brown, rusty-brown to dark brown $ X. ferrugineus f. ferrugineus

4,5 $ Context yellowish to straw-yellow but with a pale flesh rose area in the lower third of the stipe, slightly turning blue on exposure, especially above the tubes and pileus-stipe connection zone, stipe tapering downwards at maturity, basal mycelium whitish to pale yellowish
4,0 $ Context yellowish in the pileus, progressively brighter downwards, golden yellow at the base, particularly in young specimens, unchangeable on exposure, basal mycelium golden yellow, associated with Quercus spp $ X. chrysonemus

5,0 $ Pileus garnet red to blood red, this colour also occurring sometimes at the base of the stipe, associated with Quercus spp $ X. subtomentosus f. rubrotinctus
5,6 $ Pileus differently coloured, associated with several broadleaved trees

6,0 $ Pileus pure chrome yellow $ X. subtomentosus f. luteolus
6,0 $ Pileus usually brownish at first then pale ochraceous-olive but sometimes fading to yellowish- olive $ X. subtomentosus f. subtomentosus
